WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.040
Aww yeah! Today I'm going to share my&nbsp;
favorite tips to learn languages with music!&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.040
This topic is definitely my jam and&nbsp;
I have lots of experience with it.&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.000
Songs are one of my favorite&nbsp;
language learning tools!&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.560
As you will see in this lesson, they can&nbsp;
be a fun and playful way to learn English.&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.840
If you watch until the end, you&nbsp;
will get 4 fun tips and activities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.840
you can do with songs that will help you:&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:31.200
Make sense of grammatical structures, Expand&nbsp;
your vocabulary, Improve your pronunciation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.240
and of course to increase your fluency!
So, turn the volume up, and let's do this.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.520
And by the way, if you’re new here,&nbsp;
every week we help you understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.440
fast-speaking natives without getting&nbsp;
lost, without missing the jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:54.000
and without subtitles. Just like Daniel who’s&nbsp;
understanding is getting better lesson by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.560
lesson! We’re going to take your comprehension&nbsp;
to the next level, too. Just hit the subscribe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.480
button and the bell down below and you&nbsp;
won’t miss a single one of our new lessons!

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.960
So first off they can help&nbsp;
you to make sense of grammar.&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.160
You don't need to depend only on&nbsp;
books to learn grammatical rules.&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.080
Songs can also be a fun way&nbsp;
to learn or review grammar.&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.880
It helps you learn the conjugations and master&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.600
verb tenses as they are often repeated&nbsp;
over and over again in different songs.&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.120
Also, they are a great way to&nbsp;
recall the grammar you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.960
learned. You can try to spot a certain conditional&nbsp;
sentence or a relative clause, for example.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.080
So Here's one activity to use a&nbsp;
song to help you with grammar.&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.040
Choose a song you are already quite familiar with&nbsp;
and a verb tense that you feel you need to master.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.960
For example, the simple&nbsp;
past, or the participle form.&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.360
Get the lyrics of your chosen song and identify&nbsp;
all the verbs. I might highlight them in a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:59.120
color for example. This step alone is already a&nbsp;
great exercise! But let’s take it a step further!&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.560
So now, change all of them to the simple past.
At this moment, you are practicing repeating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.840
form, so don't mind if the lyrics&nbsp;
turn out to be a little strange.&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.240
Alternate between singing the song with&nbsp;
the original verbs and the changed verbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.880
a couple of times. You'll see that it'll&nbsp;
help you remember them much more easily!&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:24.160
You can do the very same activity with pretty much&nbsp;
any word family, and change adjectives to adverbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.080
add prefixes or suffixes to nouns, play&nbsp;
with the opposites of adjectives, and so on.&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.840
This might even get you laughing as you will&nbsp;
get a pretty silly new version of the song.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.560
The music is really great&nbsp;
for expand your vocabulary!&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.000
Music is a really powerful workout for your brain&nbsp;
as it activates both sides at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.800
helping increase learning and memory.
Take for example that it's mainly through songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.720
that kids learn their first pieces of vocabulary.&nbsp;
Just think about it: The ABC's, parts of the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.440
colors, actions. There are songs for all&nbsp;
of these that we learn at an early age!&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.840
Songs will help you expand your vocabulary.
It's not for nothing that Bob Dylan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.560
a musician, won the Nobel Prize in Literature&nbsp;
for his poetic expressions through music.&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.200
One of the many good things about learning&nbsp;
new words with songs is the fact that they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.280
presented in context. So, you don't learn isolated&nbsp;
items, but rather a particular meaning of a word.&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.000
Moreover, oftentimes, you won't even need&nbsp;
to look up that word in the dictionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.320
and will be able to understand it because&nbsp;
of its context, the words that came before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.800
or even the overall tone of the song.
Develop a habit of adding the words you learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.720
to your lexical book or vocabulary app, like&nbsp;
Anki, Quizlet, or Memrise, and revisiting them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.240
from time to time. Also, remember to put them into&nbsp;
use as soon as you learn them whenever possible.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.800
So, a great exercise for you to start doing&nbsp;
this right now is training with the lyrics.&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.800
One type of lyrics training is an exercise in&nbsp;
which you need to fill in the blanks of a song.&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.480
There are two ways to go about it. First is by&nbsp;
using the app or website called Lyrics Training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.840
or finding songs activities on CoolEnglish.net.
In these websites, the words you'll need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.960
identify and remember are already chosen for you,&nbsp;
although you can choose the level of difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.080
(easy, medium, hard). So it’s great&nbsp;
whether you’re a beginner or advanced!&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.520
What’s more, it's a good way to practice both&nbsp;
listening and vocabulary at the same time.&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.120
The other way is to use the principle of lyrics&nbsp;
training to help you learn through spaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.680
repetition, which is a way of learning in which&nbsp;
you take the information that you need to memorize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.320
and repeat it across increasing intervals.
You get the lyrics of the song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.000
choose words that are new to you or&nbsp;
that you have a hard time remembering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.160
and take them out of the song any time they&nbsp;
appear. After that, you play the song and try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.800
to listen and complete it with those words. After&nbsp;
a while, you may increase the challenge and try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.040
to remember the words before listening to the&nbsp;
song. This is a great way to train your ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.680
to recall words, adding them to your active&nbsp;
vocabulary. Plus, it’s such a fun challenge!&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.800
The key here is to do that in intervals (just&nbsp;
like you would with an app like Anki or Memrise).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.640
So for example, you do it first once every day,&nbsp;
then every two days, then once a week. This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.080
you're "forced" to remember those&nbsp;
words from time, so it'll be much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.240
more unlikely that you will forget them.
Once you’ve done this with a few songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.800
you can just change the song you work on each&nbsp;
day. And it will only take you a few minutes!&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.160
And I have a question--do you usually get&nbsp;
frustrated when you can’t think of the right word?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.080
If you’re like most learners I’m sure&nbsp;
you emphatically have said, “YES!!!”

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.560
Well I have two suggestions for you.
First, check out our Free Masterclass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.400
In it, you will learn the 3 keys to&nbsp;
understanding natives at any speed!&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.240
First is Vocabulary - you will&nbsp;
learn the vocabulary that we natives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.720
REALLY use in our everyday speech,&nbsp;
and we’ll help you to never forget it.&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.040
Second is Pronunciation - you&nbsp;
will understand and even be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.880
to imitate how we cut and connect our words
Third is Cultural Context - so you will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.200
able to laugh along with all of the joke
But you will not find this masterclass on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.680
YouTube. To get exclusive access you need&nbsp;
to click up here or in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.600
below to sign up for free. See you there!
The second suggestion is to check out this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.160
lesson that Andrea made on our other channel&nbsp;
about how to think in English! You will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.840
find a link to that in the description,&nbsp;
and it’s a great lesson to watch next!

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:28.960
When it comes down to it, language is music! Every&nbsp;
language has its own rhythm. If you speak English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.000
with the rhythm of your native language, it&nbsp;
can make you sound unnatural. Songs tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.480
exaggerate the rhythm of the language, so&nbsp;
they can be a great tool to help you practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.960
speaking English with the correct rhythm&nbsp;
and flow, while stressing words correctly!&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.440
Of course, because of the melody in some songs,&nbsp;
there might be slight changes in pronunciation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:54.080
This is however the exception, so it should&nbsp;
not scare you away from learning with songs.&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.920
Most of the time, words that rhyme fall&nbsp;
on the same beat (or pulse) of the song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.560
and this certainly helps you identify&nbsp;
patterns on how to pronounce such words.&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.560
It's also helpful for you to learn&nbsp;
how to actually pronounce a word.&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:14.160
Sometimes we learn words by reading and don't&nbsp;
know how to pronounce them. Or worse, get bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:20.320
habits of pronouncing them incorrectly! Songs will&nbsp;
help you with that in a natural and enjoyable way.&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.200
Another thing is that songs are enriched&nbsp;
with is connected speech and they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.480
help you notice when and which words&nbsp;
are commonly reduced and linked.&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.120
One of the benefits of using songs&nbsp;
to study is accent reduction.&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.000
Have you ever noticed how singers with a very&nbsp;
strong British accent like Adele or Ed Sheeran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.400
sound kind of American when they sing?
This happens because accents are distinguished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.560
based on the use of intonation, vowel length,&nbsp;
articulation, and so on. However, when a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.240
sings, these aspects are neutralized, as the air&nbsp;
flows and the articulation and length of syllables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.200
and sounds tend to become less precise.
What’s more, the muscles being articulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.320
to project the voice of a particular genre,&nbsp;
let's say pop music, tend to share the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:06.640
features of a neutral American accent.
So singing can sort of take your mouth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.320
to the gym because it uses all the&nbsp;
parts of your body that produce sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.600
and helps you exaggerate the difficult phonemes&nbsp;
in English that don’t exist in your native tongue.&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.640
A good exercise to practice pronunciation,&nbsp;
inspired by the Mimic Method, is to first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:25.760
try to imitate the sounds you hear in&nbsp;
the song without caring about the lyrics.&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:31.040
Just say random, invented words (also&nbsp;
known as gibberish) in the same rhythm and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:37.440
melody of the song, if possible. Identify&nbsp;
when a word is emphasized, when a sound rises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.160
and how many sounds there are in a sentence.
This will help warm up the muscles so when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:46.800
you actually pronounce the real words of the&nbsp;
lyrics, they fall into place more comfortably.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.080
So if you still aren’t convinced about&nbsp;
the benefits of songs for learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:00.160
English--let me give you a final reason!
Music is bound to help your fluency in general.&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.520
As I mentioned, music has been one of my number&nbsp;
one language learning tool in most of the 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.960
languages I have learned. I created playlists in&nbsp;
Spotify for each language. I might listen to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.600
French playlist while cooking, or Catalan&nbsp;
playlist while working. By singing along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.480
I noticed huge differences in the naturalness&nbsp;
of my speech when I get into a conversation.&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.960
Why is this?
Well first, because music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:28.160
affects our mood and it can help create a better&nbsp;
atmosphere for learning. It might sound silly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.960
but having the right mindset does wonders for&nbsp;
our motivation. Which sounds better to you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.240
Studying lyrics or studying a grammar book?
Second, when we like a song, we tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:43.280
stick with it for a while, listening&nbsp;
to it on repeat non-stop. Repetition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.480
even from a passive perspective, is an extremely&nbsp;
effective way of increasing memorization.&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.280
You can use melodies that you already know or&nbsp;
that are stuck in your head and change the lyrics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.600
Invent your own story to the song, invert&nbsp;
the meaning of everything that's being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.334
you can create movements to words&nbsp;
in the song or to certain momentums.&nbsp;

00:10:00.334 --> 00:10:02.720
&gt;&gt;&gt;
But, the most important thing is to SING&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.760
ALONG! Enjoy the moment! Sing out loud.
Be it in your car on the way to work, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:13.120
the shower, or my favorite--while cooking dinner!
If you want to take it to the next level try this:&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.280
Download a program like Audacity&nbsp;
(which is free) to edit audio.&nbsp;

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:22.400
Cut the song into small pieces, even just&nbsp;
a few seconds. Choose especially the parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:28.080
that you find most challenging to imitate.
Listen to these bits over and over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:32.080
Once you feel comfortable identifying&nbsp;
what’s being said, sing those small bits.&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.920
Think of it like reps with a dumbbell&nbsp;
that you’d use to make your arms stronger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.360
but for your muscles of articulation!
If this is difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.120
you can also start by slowing down the audio.
Then once you get more comfortable, speed it up!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.360
This will help you speak English FASTER!
If you want a good challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.600
check out how we broke down Eminem’s&nbsp;
song Lose Yourself in this lesson!&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:56.560
By trying this exercise and&nbsp;
others that we’ve mentioned today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:02.320
before you know it, you’re going to be speaking&nbsp;
English clearly and naturally. Aww yeah!

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.000
So to wrap up this lesson, I have&nbsp;
some lightning recommendations for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.880
that will make all of this more fun and easier!&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.200
So first off, start with songs you like, but that&nbsp;
are suitable for learning and for your level.&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.400
Don’t choose a song just because&nbsp;
a teacher told you it’s a good one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.400
if you don’t like it. Otherwise you will&nbsp;
not be motivated and will probably give up.&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:31.760
If you need some help finding a song, a&nbsp;
great place to start is with our playlist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.240
of lessons with songs. You can find that by&nbsp;
clicking up here and also in the description.&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.120
Make sure you have access to the lyrics. I&nbsp;
really like Genius Lyrics because you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.120
also see information about what the song&nbsp;
is about, and sometimes this can help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.400
understand the cultural context of them. As&nbsp;
a bonus, print them out, take notes, and mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.720
them up. Use colors! Be creative!
Mix active listening activities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.040
like the exercises we mentioned with passive&nbsp;
listening, that is, listening for pleasure.

